WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:05.479
Some of The Rise of Skywalker's biggest surprises
happen in a lightspeed flash.

00:00:05.479 --> 00:00:09.510
That's why we're here to run down the biggest
surprise cameos in the movie, from original

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:12.450
trilogy sidekicks to behind-the-camera architects.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:18.800
"The dead speak!" proclaims The Rise of Skywalker's
opening crawl, making it clear as soon as

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.940
possible that things are about to get weird.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:26.819
And weird they do indeed get, as we find Kylo
Ren arriving at the planet Exegol in response

00:00:26.819 --> 00:00:32.509
to a mysterious broadcast from the (somehow
alive) Sith Lord and Emperor Sheev Palpatine,

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:37.360
a broadcast heard on planet Earth exclusively
as a Fortnite cross-promotion...

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.660
"At last the work of generations is complete.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.090
The great error is corrected."

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:50.760
Ren finds Palpatine, but that's no surprise,
we've known since the movie's first trailer

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.200
that he'd be back.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:57.010
More unexpected are the cameos from other
past antagonists, particularly because of

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.150
the unusual form they take.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:03.420
When Palpatine introduces himself as "every
voice you've ever heard inside your head,"

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:08.440
his voice shapeshifts briefly into that of
Supreme Leader Snoke, and then that of Darth

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:09.440
Vader.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.570
Sure enough, both Andy Serkis and James Earl
Jones lend their menacing pipes to the illusion.

00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:19.010
Snoke even appears a moment later in the flesh,
nothing but the flesh, actually, with parts

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:24.659
of seemingly rejected clone bodies floating
in a tank like a savory Snoke stew.

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:28.830
It's surprising to see Harrison Ford in Rise
of Skywalker for a few reasons.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:33.940
Sure, there's the fact that non-Jedi have
never appeared as "Force Ghosts" in the movies

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:38.729
before, an issue the film skirts by presenting
Han Solo as a figment of his wayward son's

00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:41.130
memory, rather than an actual specter.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:44.440
There's also Ford's infamous annoyance with
the legacy of Star Wars.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:49.380
He unsuccessfully campaigned to get Solo killed
off in Return of the Jedi and will take great

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.580
pleasure in telling you he doesn't care who
shot first.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:56.390
It was surprising enough when it was first
announced that he would star in The Force

00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:01.120
Awakens, and downright shocking to see him
pop up again in Rise of Skywalker.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.520
But Ford seems to enjoy working with Adam
Driver, and we can guess he probably liked

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.900
the idea of paying tribute to Carrie Fisher.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:12.400
That's why it's easy to overlook what could've
been eye-roll-worthy fan service.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.340
From the moment we knew that The Rise of Skywalker
would revisit the Endor system, we had to

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.099
wonder if we'd be seeing some Ewoks.

00:02:19.099 --> 00:02:23.200
Sadly, the wreckage of the second Death Star
isn't actually on the same forest moon we

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.819
visited in Return of the Jedi, but on Endor's
"ocean moon."

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:31.450
The heroes' big knife chase brings them into
contact with their new friend Jannah and her

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:35.640
very good space horses, but sadly, the adventure
remains devoid of killer bears.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:39.099
That is, at least, until the movie's grand
finale.

00:02:39.099 --> 00:02:43.550
As the Final Order fleet falls, a montage
much like the one added to the "Special Edition"

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:49.050
of Return of the Jedi brings us to a few planets
to witness the galactic victory celebration.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:53.770
That's when we're reintroduced to Wicket,
played once again by Star Wars mainstay Warwick

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:58.220
Davis, who was just 11 years old the first
time he got zipped up into a teddy bear costume

00:02:58.220 --> 00:02:59.550
for Episode 6.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:05.090
Alongside him is Wicket's son, Pommet, played
by Warwick's real-life son, Harrison.

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:08.290
Wedge Antilles is something of a Star Wars
good luck charm.

00:03:08.290 --> 00:03:12.560
Having survived all three major battles of
the original trilogy, he was always there

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.959
when Luke and the Rebel Alliance needed him,
even if he never took up a lot of screentime.

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:21.600
The character would go on to play a major
role in plenty of Expanded Universe material,

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.160
endearing him to a generation of superfans.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:28.290
It was surprising, then, that Wedge didn't
make an appearance in The Force Awakens.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:32.739
At the time, actor Denis Lawson was quoted
as saying he turned down an offer to appear

00:03:32.739 --> 00:03:35.260
because it would have, quote, "bored" him.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:39.170
The actor later backtracked these comments,
claiming that he would have accepted the role

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:41.300
if not for scheduling conflicts.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:46.370
Whatever the case, he's there for a brief
moment in Rise of Skywalker's climactic space

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:49.840
battle, manning a gun turret on the Millennium
Falcon.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.129
It's Star Wars tradition for behind-the-scenes
crewmembers to make blink-and-you'll-miss-them

00:03:54.129 --> 00:03:55.670
on screen cameos.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:04:00.260
The Rise of Skywalker continues this tradition
of giving incidental background roles to hardworking

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:01.540
creative types.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:07.739
Director J.J. Abrams provides the stuttering
voice of skittish droid D-O, while his Episode

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:14.190
9 co-writer Chris Terrio can be heard as Aftab
Ackbar, the squidlike son of the beloved admiral

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:17.100
who perished during the events of The Last
Jedi.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:22.910
Most significantly, composer John Williams
can briefly be seen as Oma Tres, the bartender

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:27.020
at the Kijimi dive where our heroes find Babu
Frik.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:31.460
As The Rise of Skywalker begins, we learn
that Rey has been continuing her Jedi training

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:36.690
under Leia, focusing specifically on the effort
to commune with the spirits of past Jedi.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:40.520
Communication through the Force has been a
specialty of Leia's ever since The Empire

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:43.780
Strikes Back, before she was even aware of
her own abilities.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:50.051
Still, despite her training, Rey remains frustrated
and unable to hear any voices...until her

00:04:50.051 --> 00:04:55.740
most desperate moment during the film's climax,
as she lies nearly defeated at the feet of

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:56.740
Palpatine.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:01.139
That's when generations of Jedi finally talk
back, offering her encouragement and support.

00:05:01.139 --> 00:05:07.680
Some, like Yoda, Anakin, Mace Windu, Obi-Wan
Kenobi, and Qui-Gonn Jinn, are pretty obvious,

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.270
but in the chaos of the big final battle it
was probably hard to make all those voice

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:13.610
cameos out.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:18.440
Here's a quick rundown of the more obscure
"Voices of Jedi Past" featured in this sequence:

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:23.860
Adi Gallia first appeared in The Phantom Menace,
but most fans know her best from the animated

00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:26.360
Star Wars: The Clone Wars series.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:32.120
Similarly, Luminara Unduli and Aayla Secura
first appeared in Attack of the Clones, but

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.470
are both best known from Clone Wars.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:40.280
Ahsoka Tano is also from Clone Wars, but The
Rise of Skywalker is the character's live-action

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:43.780
debut, technically, even if we only hear her
voice.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:48.759
Same goes for Kanan Jarrus from Star Wars
Rebels, meaning former teen heartthrob Freddie

00:05:48.759 --> 00:05:53.310
Prinze Jr., or his voice, at least, is in
The Rise of Skywalker.

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:54.960
Talk about a surprising cameo...

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:55.960
"What was that?"

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:56.960
"I was busy."

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:57.960
"Yeah, busy wigging."

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.169
"Excuse me, I did not wig."

00:06:00.169 --> 00:06:01.990
"There was major wiggage."

